   "source": [
    "# 05 Â· Hypnodensityâ€“feature correlations\n",
    "\n",
    "This notebook is a focused derivative of `03_feature_associations.ipynb`. It reuses the\n",
    "same validated feature/hypnodensity assembly, but replaces the treatment-effect pipeline\n",
    "with a direct, descriptive correlation analysis between raw epoch-level EEG features and\n",
    "raw epoch-level hypnodensity probabilities.\n",
    "\n",
    "The analysis unit throughout is the **epoch** (one row per recording Ã— block Ã— epoch Ã—\n",
    "electrode Ã— stager, after joining monoelectrode features onto the aligned staging output).\n",
    "Four correlation analyses are run, each pooling data differently:\n",
    "\n",
    "1. **Global** â€” one feature Ã— probability correlation matrix per stager, pooled across all\n",
    "   subjects, epochs, electrodes, experimental phases and doses. This looks for a\n",
    "   featureâ€“probability mapping that holds on average across the whole dataset.\n",
    "2. **Per electrode** â€” the same matrix, repeated separately for every electrode. This asks\n",
    "   whether the global mapping found in (1) actually depends on which electrode is used.\n",
    "3. **Per phase Ã— dose segment** â€” the same global matrix, computed separately within each\n",
    "   of the four `experimental_label Ã— condition` segments (before-placebo, before-DMT,\n",
    "   after-placebo, after-DMT). This asks whether the featureâ€“probability mapping itself\n",
    "   shifts depending on where in the experiment (and under which drug condition) it is\n",
    "   estimated â€” i.e. whether the statistical model implicitly partitions its input space.\n",
    "4. **Per electrode, per phase Ã— dose segment** â€” analysis (3) repeated separately for every\n",
    "   electrode, to check whether that partitioning itself is electrode-dependent.\n",
    "\n",
    "Spearman correlation is used throughout: hypnodensity probabilities are bounded and often\n",
    "far from normally distributed, and several EEG features are heavy-tailed, so a rank-based,\n",
    "monotonic measure is a safer default than Pearson's r."
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "## i) Imports"
   ]
  },
  {
